{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>Whoever does not uphold the words of this Torah</b> — here He encompassed the entire Torah, and they accepted it upon themselves with an oath and imprecation; this is the language of Rashi. In my opinion, this acceptance refers to one who acknowledges the commandments in his heart and regards them as truth, and believes that one who performs them will have reward and good, while one who transgresses them will be punished — and if he denies one of them, or regards it as permanently nullified, behold, he is cursed. But if he transgressed one of them, such as one who ate pork or an abomination out of desire, or who failed to make a sukkah or take a lulav out of laziness — he is not included in this ban, for Scripture did not say 'whoever does not do the words of this Torah,' but rather said 'whoever does not uphold the words of this Torah, to do them' — in the sense of 'the Jews established and accepted' (Esther 9:27). This, then, is the ban against rebels and heretics.\n\nAnd in the Jerusalem Talmud in Sotah (7:4) I found: 'Whoever does not uphold' — but is there a Torah that falls? Rabbi Shimon ben Yakim says: this refers to the synagogue attendant. Rabbi Shimon ben Chalaphta says: this refers to a lower court. For Mar Rav Yehudah and Rav Huna in the name of Shmuel: on account of this matter Josiah rent his garments and said, 'upon me to uphold.' Rabbi Asi said in the name of Rabbi Tanḥum bar Ḥiyya: one who learned and taught and observed and performed, and it was within his power to uphold [the Torah] and he did not uphold it — behold, this one is included in 'cursed.' They interpret this 'upholding' as referring to the royal house and the leadership, for it is in their power to uphold the Torah against those who nullify it; and even if he himself were perfectly righteous in his deeds, and he was able to uphold the Torah against the wicked who nullify it but did not — behold, he is cursed. And this is close to the interpretation we have given.\n\nAnd they said by way of aggadah: this refers to the synagogue attendant who does not uphold the scrolls of the Torah to stand them upright according to their proper procedure so that they do not fall. And it appears to me regarding the attendant that it refers to one who does not lift up a Torah scroll before the congregation to show the face of its writing to all, as is explained in Masekhet Soferim (14:14): that he raises it and shows the face of its writing to the people standing to his right and to his left, and turns it to his front and to his behind — for it is a mitzvah upon all, men and women, to see the writing and to bow and to say, 'and this is the Torah that Moses set' (Deuteronomy 4:44), and so is the custom."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {0 "The Jerusalem Talmud passage includes the cryptic phrase 'דמר רב יהודה ורב הונא בשם שמואל' which appears to introduce the statement about Josiah; I have rendered it hyper-literally as it stands in the text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:56:20.721642429Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}